    "['ip address unnumbered interface [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces lo0 unit 0 family inet unnumbered-address', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Huawei command `ip address unnumbered interface LoopBack1`:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces lo0 unit 0 family inet unnumbered-address\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper:\\n1. `lo0` is the default loopback interface (equivalent to Huawei's `LoopBack1`)\\n2. The unnumbered configuration is applied at the protocol family level (family inet)\\n3. This assumes you've already configured the loopback address on lo0 (equivalent to Huawei's `interface LoopBack1` configuration)\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces lo0 unit 0 family inet unnumbered-address'}]]": 1,
    "['tunnel-protocol [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls interface ge-2/0/0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent of the HUAWEI command `tunnel-protocol mpls te`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls interface ge-2/0/0\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1 to 4.4.4.4\\n##\\n\\nOr more precisely for just the tunnel-protocol specification (without the destination):\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1 mpls-te\\n##\\n\\nNote: Juniper doesn't have an exact 1:1 command for `tunnel-protocol mpls te` since the concept is implemented differently. The MPLS TE tunnel configuration in Juniper is spread across multiple configuration statements under `protocols mpls`. The first example shows the complete tunnel setup equivalent, while the second shows the closest direct translation of just the protocol specification.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls interface ge-2/0/0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1 to 4.4.4.4', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent of the HUAWEI command `tunnel-protocol mpls te`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls interface ge-2/0/0\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1 to 4.4.4.4\\n##\\n\\nOr more precisely for just the tunnel-protocol specification (without the destination):\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1 mpls-te\\n##\\n\\nNote: Juniper doesn't have an exact 1:1 command for `tunnel-protocol mpls te` since the concept is implemented differently. The MPLS TE tunnel configuration in Juniper is spread across multiple configuration statements under `protocols mpls`. The first example shows the complete tunnel setup equivalent, while the second shows the closest direct translation of just the protocol specification.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1 to 4.4.4.4'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1 mpls-te', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent of the HUAWEI command `tunnel-protocol mpls te`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls interface ge-2/0/0\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1 to 4.4.4.4\\n##\\n\\nOr more precisely for just the tunnel-protocol specification (without the destination):\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1 mpls-te\\n##\\n\\nNote: Juniper doesn't have an exact 1:1 command for `tunnel-protocol mpls te` since the concept is implemented differently. The MPLS TE tunnel configuration in Juniper is spread across multiple configuration statements under `protocols mpls`. The first example shows the complete tunnel setup equivalent, while the second shows the closest direct translation of just the protocol specification.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1 mpls-te'}]]": 1,
    "['destination [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path LSP1 to 4.4.4.4', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the HUAWEI command `destination 4.4.4.4` for an MPLS TE tunnel:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path LSP1 to 4.4.4.4\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, MPLS TE tunnels are configured as \"label-switched-path\" (LSP) objects rather than tunnel interfaces. The `to` statement specifies the destination address, which is equivalent to HUAWEI\\'s `destination` parameter. You would typically name the LSP (e.g., \"LSP1\") according to your naming conventions.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path LSP1 to 4.4.4.4'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls te tunnel-id [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1-to-4.4.4.4 template', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Juniper equivalent command for `mpls te tunnel-id 100`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1-to-4.4.4.4 template\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1-to-4.4.4.4 to 4.4.4.4\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1-to-4.4.4.4 lsp-id 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: Juniper doesn\\'t use tunnel IDs in exactly the same way as Huawei. Instead:\\n1. The LSP name is user-defined (I used \"Tunnel1-to-4.4.4.4\" to match the Huawei context)\\n2. The destination matches the Huawei tunnel destination (4.4.4.4)\\n3. The lsp-id in Juniper serves the same purpose as the tunnel-id in Huawei for identifying the LSP', 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1-to-4.4.4.4 template'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1-to-4.4.4.4 to 4.4.4.4', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Juniper equivalent command for `mpls te tunnel-id 100`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1-to-4.4.4.4 template\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1-to-4.4.4.4 to 4.4.4.4\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1-to-4.4.4.4 lsp-id 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: Juniper doesn\\'t use tunnel IDs in exactly the same way as Huawei. Instead:\\n1. The LSP name is user-defined (I used \"Tunnel1-to-4.4.4.4\" to match the Huawei context)\\n2. The destination matches the Huawei tunnel destination (4.4.4.4)\\n3. The lsp-id in Juniper serves the same purpose as the tunnel-id in Huawei for identifying the LSP', 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1-to-4.4.4.4 to 4.4.4.4'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1-to-4.4.4.4 lsp-id 100', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Juniper equivalent command for `mpls te tunnel-id 100`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1-to-4.4.4.4 template\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1-to-4.4.4.4 to 4.4.4.4\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1-to-4.4.4.4 lsp-id 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: Juniper doesn\\'t use tunnel IDs in exactly the same way as Huawei. Instead:\\n1. The LSP name is user-defined (I used \"Tunnel1-to-4.4.4.4\" to match the Huawei context)\\n2. The destination matches the Huawei tunnel destination (4.4.4.4)\\n3. The lsp-id in Juniper serves the same purpose as the tunnel-id in Huawei for identifying the LSP', 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel1-to-4.4.4.4 lsp-id 100'}]]": 1,
